    .mjpage__block {\n                            text-align: center;\n                            margin: 1em 0em;\n                            position: relative;\n                            display: block!important;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            width: 100%\n                            }</style><p>First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n<span class=\"mjpage mjpage__block\"><svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"42.003ex\" height=\"3.176ex\" style=\"vertical-align: -1.005ex;\" viewBox=\"0 -934.9 18084.7 1367.4\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-labelledby=\"MathJax-SVG-1-Title\">\n<title id=\"MathJax-SVG-1-Title\">\n\\lnot (\\lnot Y \\Leftrightarrow Z) \n\\equiv \\lnot \\big[(\\lnot Y \\Rightarrow Z) \\land (Z \\Rightarrow \\lnot Y)\\big]\n</title>\n<defs aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<path stroke-width=\"1\" id=\"E1-MJMAIN-AC\" d=\"M56 323T56 336T70 356H596Q603 353 611 343V102Q598 89 591 89Q587 89 584 90T579 94T575 98T572 102L571 209V316H70Q56 323 56 336Z\"></path>\n<path stroke-width=\"1\" id=\"E1-MJMAIN-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path>\n<path stroke-width=\"1\" id=\"E1-MJMATHI-59\" d=\"M66 637Q54 637 49 637T39 638T32 641T30 647T33 664T42 682Q44 683 56 683Q104 680 165 680Q288 680 306 683H316Q322 677 322 674T320 656Q316 643 310 637H298Q242 637 242 624Q242 619 292 477T343 333L346 336Q350 340 358 349T379 373T411 410T454 461Q546 568 561 587T577 618Q577 634 545 637Q528 637 528 647Q528 64